From dc17e26296c5947834f13ea1b0a4e8b72b6dfb79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:36:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fast path covers parameter links: amplitude modulation no
 longer falls back to JAX

A setup may derive one parameter from another through a linking function. The
public aLIGO and UIFO constructors do that in their amplitude modulation mode
(signal amplitude = sqrt(laser power)). fast.supports() turned every setup
with a link away, so such a setup ran through the dense JAX implementation:
9.7 s instead of 0.4 s for the first run of the aLIGO benchmark pipeline with
mode="amplitude_modulation" on an M4 (compilation alone 9.4 s).

Links whose function is in LINK_RULES (jnp.sqrt, the only one the public
setups use) are now evaluated on the host: EntryPlan._row applies them to the
parameter row after the design variables and before the sweeps, with every
link reading the row as it was before any of them wrote, as simulate_jax
does. A linked slot passes derivatives on to its source slot, and the
pullback moves its cotangent there with the derivative of the linking
function. The phase of a balanced homodyne detector is read after the links.
Any other linking function, or linking function indices that are only known
at run time, still use simulate_jax.

Against simulate_jax (runs/check_setups.py, aLIGO amplitude modulation, all
46 parameters as design variables): carrier 6.7e-13, signal 5.4e-12, noise
3.1e-12, sensitivity 1.8e-08, gradient 1.7e-09. Setups without links are
untouched: the outputs of benchmark.py and benchmark_aligo.py are
bit-identical, all scripts under runs/philip and runs/flags pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
